-- title:
--   The commutator subgroup of $F$
-- statement:
--   The commutator subgroup $[F,F]$ of Thompson's group $F$ consists of exactly those
--   elements of $F$ which are trivial in a neighborhood of $0$ and in a neighborhood of $1$.
--
--   An element is trivial in a neighborhood of $0$ if there is $\varepsilon > 0$ such that it
--   fixes every point of $[0,\varepsilon)$, and trivial in a neighborhood of $1$ if there is
--   $\varepsilon > 0$ such that it fixes every point of $(1-\varepsilon, 1]$. The two radii are
--   chosen independently. The commutator subgroup is the subgroup generated by all commutators
--   $ghg^{-1}h^{-1}$, not merely the set of commutators.
-- source:
--   Cannon, J. W., Floyd, W. J., Parry, W. R., Introductory notes on Richard Thompson's groups, L'Enseignement Mathematique (2) 42 (1996) 215-256, https://doi.org/10.5169/seals-87877, Theorem 4.1, p. 228, first sentence

import Definitions.Def_CannonFloydParry
